WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.780
Nature is full of organisms that iridesce

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.960
in wild displays of structural color

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.940
and some can even control it themselves.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.160
While dyes and pigments produce colour

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.420
by selectively absorbing light, structural colors

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.420
are produced when light interacts with the

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.600
microscopic structure of a material.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.660
Inspired by an organism that uses structural color,

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:27.680
the chameleon, researchers have created a biohybrid--

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.500
a color-changing gel embedded with living heart cells

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.820
that can display and control structural color on its own.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.620
Their method involves placing heart muscle cells,

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.620
cardiomyocytes, onto biocompatible

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:44.820
gels with structural color properties.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.780
The gels were made by arranging silica nanoparticles

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.180
on a glass slide and covering them with a

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:52.680
specialized gelatin.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.300
This gel was then peeled off,

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.100
leaving imprints of tiny pores from the nanoparticles.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.880
Some wavelengths of light disappeared

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.900
down into the pores, while others were reflected,

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.440
creating vivid colors.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.940
When the heart cells swell and contract,

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.800
the gel does the same,

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.360
resulting in a shift in its structural color,

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.800
in synchrony with the pulsing of the cells.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.220
By placing cells into tiny patterned grooves in the gel,

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:20.500
the team was able to create a material

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.920
that mimics a tiny butterfly flapping its wings.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.680
As the cells expand and contract, the wings move,

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.740
displaying a changing structural color.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.400
The team also showed that the biohybrid gels

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.720
could be combined with other materials to

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.660
mimic the functions of the heart

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:40.600
as a virtual “heart on a chip.”

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.460
Such a device could be used for biomedical testing

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.680
instead of using human or animal organs.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.820
They tested the heart-on-chip by adding

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.040
various concentrations of a drug

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.680
which is known to change the heart rate.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.080
Different concentrations of the drug

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.920
made the heart cells beat at different frequencies

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.659
and produce shifts in the structural colour of the gel.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.800
This platform could make it easier to monitor

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:08.100
the effects of drugs in testing using only the naked eye.

